<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1912</h1><p class="ref-lede">1912 in world history: 25 events, 23 births, 9 deaths, 1 work, 9 states founded and 6 that came to an end. 750 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a><a href="/years/1909">1909</a><a href="/years/1910">1910</a><a href="/years/1911">1911</a><a href="/years/1912" aria-current="page">1912</a><a href="/years/1913">1913</a><a href="/years/1914">1914</a><a href="/years/1915">1915</a><a href="/years/1916">1916</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Sun Yat-sen proclaims the Republic of China in Nanjing</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> was sworn in as provisional president of the new Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Six weeks later the last Qing emperor abdicated, ending more than two thousand years of imperial rule.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankfurt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-6">6 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Alfred Wegener presents his theory of continental drift</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At a geology meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, Wegener argued that the continents had once formed a single landmass and drifted apart. Long rejected, the idea was vindicated by plate tectonics in the 1960s.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-12">12 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Puyi, the last Emperor of China, abdicates</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Empress Dowager Longyu issued the edict of abdication for the six-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a>. The fall of the Qing ended more than two thousand years of imperial rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Juliette Gordon Low founds the Girl Scouts in Savannah</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Inspired by the Guide movement she had seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Low gathered 18 girls for the first meeting of the American Girl Guides. Renamed the Girl Scouts in 1913, it has had tens of millions of members.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-10">10 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Titanic leaves Southampton on her maiden voyage</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">RMS Titanic, the largest ship afloat, sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> via Cherbourg and Queenstown. Five days later she struck an iceberg and sank with the loss of more than 1,500 lives.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Titanic strikes an iceberg in the North Atlantic</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At 11.40 pm the liner struck an iceberg south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, opening several forward compartments to the sea. She sank less than three hours later, early on 15 April.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-15">15 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Titanic sinks with the loss of more than 1,500 lives</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The liner went down at 2.20 am with lifeboat places for barely half of the 2,224 aboard. The disaster led to the first International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea in 1914.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-5">5 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Woodrow Wilson is elected US president</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a> splitting the Republican vote, the Democrat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> won the presidency. He created the Federal Reserve and led America into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-12">12 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Searchers find the tent of Scott's polar party</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A search party found the snow-covered tent containing the bodies of Robert Falcon Scott and two companions, eight months after their deaths returning from the South Pole. Scott's diary told their story.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Albania declares independence</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-qemali">Ismail Qemali</a> raised the Albanian flag and proclaimed independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>. The great powers recognised the new state the following year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">'Piltdown Man' is presented to the Geological Society</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Charles Dawson and Arthur Smith Woodward announced fossils from Piltdown in Sussex as an early human ancestor. In 1953 they were exposed as a forgery combining a human skull and an orang-utan jaw.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/saenz-pena-law" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saenz-pena-law.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/saenz-pena-law">Sáenz Peña Law</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Sáenz Peña Law was the Argentine electoral law of 1912, promoted by President Roque Sáenz Peña, which established secret, universal and compulsory male suffrage for citizens and representation for minorities.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fez">Treaty of Fez</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of Fez was signed on 30 March 1912 by Sultan Abd al-Hafid of Morocco and France after the Agadir Crisis.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ouchy" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ouchy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ouchy">Treaty of Ouchy</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of Ouchy, signed at Ouchy near Lausanne on 18 October 1912, ended the Italo-Turkish War.</p></div></li></ol></section>
